$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Chiral imidazolinium salt (9). The Boc-diamine (1.02 mg, 1.67 mmol) was
dissolved in methanol (16 mL) 78 °C. Anhydrous HCl was gently bubbled
through the solution for 5 min. The reaction was then allowed to warm to
22 °C and the solvent was removed at reduced pressure. This delivered an
off-white solid, which was rigorously dried (12 h) under vacuum in an
Abderhalden pistol containing P2O5 over refluxing isopropanol. The
off-white solid was suspended in triethylorthoformate (12 mL) in a 25 mL
round bottom flask. The vessel was equipped with a short-path
distillation head and submerged into an oil bath preheated to 125 °C. The
mixture was stirred at 125 °C and the distillation head was heated
periodically (approximately every 30 min) with a heat gun until ethanol
no longer distilled over (typically 4 h). After cooling the flask to 22
°C, the mixture was diluted with 30 mL of Et2O and filtered. The
precipitate was washed with additional Et2O ( 2 x 1 0 mL) and dried under
vacuum (12 h) in an Abderhalden pistol charged with P2O5 over refluxing
isopropanol to give an off white solid (750 mg, 1.34 mmol, 81%). IR
(neat): 3371 (br), 2960 (br), 2603 (w), 1675 (w), 1630 (s), 1563 (m),
1486 (s), 1428 (w), 1354 (m), 1312 (s), 1260 (s), 1199 (s), 1168 (m),
1156 (m), 1120 (s), 1068 (m), 944 (m), 828 (m), 734 (m). 1H NMR (400 MHz,
CDCl3): δ 11.14 (br, 1H), 8.13-8.05 (m, 2H), 7.93 (d, J = 9.0 Hz, 1H),
7.81-7.71 (m, 3H), 7.50 (t, J = 7.3 Hz, 1H), 7.36 (s, 1H), 7.31-7.26 (m,
2H), 7.13 (d, J = 8.6 Hz, 1H), 6.92 (d, J = 8.6 Hz, 1H), 6.73 (br, 2H),
4.82-4.74 (m, 1H), 4.34-4.17 (m, 2H), 3.82-3.74 (m, 1H), 2.19 (s, 3H),
1.75 (br, 6H). 13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3): δ 156.1, 140.7, 135.5, 133.6,
132.9, 132.3, 131.6, 130.5, 130.0, 129.9, 129.9, 128.6, 127.8, 127.5,
127.3, 127.1, 126.6, 126.5, 126.5, 124.4, 122.9, 122.1, 122.0, 113.6,
52.3, 51.5, 21.1, 17.5. HRMS Calcd for C33H28F3N2O (M-Cl): δ25.2154.
Found: 525.2156.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Chiral Ru-Based Complexes for Asymmetric Olefin Metathesis: Enhancement
of Catalyst Activity through Steric and Electronic Modification
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
